    <h2>What I build for you with Symfony</h2>
    <p>
        Symfony is the PHP framework for applications that are meant to last: a clear structure, fixed release cycles
        with long-term support versions and an ecosystem that has been stable for many years. I have worked with it
        for more than 15 years and typically build these kinds of projects:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Internal tools and portals</strong> that replace spreadsheets, paper and copy-and-paste, for example for customer, order or appointment management.</li>
        <li><strong>Customer portals and SaaS applications</strong> with login, roles, multiple tenants and cleanly separated data.</li>
        <li><strong>Interfaces</strong> to payment providers, ERP, CRM or accounting so that data no longer has to be transferred by hand. More under <a href="{{ path('app_service_api') }}">API development and automation</a>.</li>
        <li><strong>Documents and workflows</strong>: invoices, reports and forms as PDF, email processing and scheduled background tasks.</li>
        <li><strong>AI features</strong> in existing applications, from data extraction to an assistant that knows your company, see <a href="{{ path('app_service_ai') }}">AI integration</a>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Existing applications: upgrade and takeover</h2>
    <p>
        Many enquiries are not about a new build but about an application that has been running for years. Two cases come up particularly often.
    </p>
    <h3>Upgrading from Symfony 5 or 6 to 7.4 LTS</h3>
    <p>
        Symfony 5.4 no longer receives regular bug fixes, and bug-fix support for 6.4 ends in late November 2026.
        Moving to Symfony 7.4 LTS now gives you several quiet years again. My approach is always the same: first
        remove the deprecation warnings of the current version, then make the version jump, backed by tests and
        static analysis. PHP is moved to a version that still receives security updates at the same time. The
        details are on the page <a href="{{ path('app_service_upgrade') }}">Symfony upgrade to 7.4 LTS</a>.
    </p>
    <h3>Taking over an existing project</h3>
    <p>
        The original developer has left, the agency has no capacity, or nobody dares to touch the code any more? I
        take over existing Symfony projects, even without documentation or tests. It starts with an honest
        assessment: which versions are running, where the security risks are, which parts are stable and which are
        not. Then we decide together what comes first. More under
        <a href="{{ path('app_service_maintenance') }}">taking over and maintaining Symfony projects</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>How a project works</h2>
    <ol>
        <li><strong>Initial call (free of charge)</strong>: you tell me what it is about, I ask questions and tell you honestly whether I am the right person for it.</li>
        <li><strong>Analysis and quote</strong>: for new builds we clarify scope and priorities, for existing applications I look at the code. You receive a transparent estimate.</li>
        <li><strong>Delivery in short steps</strong>: I deliver in small, testable increments on a staging environment. You see real results early instead of slides.</li>
        <li><strong>Go-live</strong>: automated deployment, backups and a clear way back in case something does not fit.</li>
        <li><strong>Operations and further development</strong>: updates, small changes and new features for as long as you want. On request I also take care of hosting and domains, see <a href="{{ path('app_service_hosting') }}">hosting from one source</a>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Technology and quality</h2>
    <p>
        I rely on proven tools rather than experiments, so that someone can still understand and extend your
        application five years from now:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Current versions</strong>: Symfony 7.4 LTS or newer, PHP 8.4 or 8.5, Doctrine ORM with MySQL, MariaDB or PostgreSQL.</li>
        <li><strong>Automated tests</strong> with PHPUnit for the workflows that matter, and static analysis with PHPStan.</li>
        <li><strong>Docker</strong> for a development environment that runs the same on every machine.</li>
        <li><strong>CI/CD</strong>: tests and security checks run on every push, deployments are automated and repeatable.</li>
        <li><strong>Your repository</strong>: the code belongs to you and is in your hands from day one.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Pricing model</h2>
    <p>
        I offer three models. Which one fits depends on how clear the scope is at the start.
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Time and materials</strong> at an hourly rate: for further development, debugging and projects whose scope changes along the way. You receive a breakdown of the hours worked.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed price</strong>: for clearly defined packages, such as an upgrade analysis or an upgrade with a known scope. The price is agreed upfront.</li>
        <li><strong>Maintenance</strong> with a fixed monthly budget: for applications that regularly need updates and small adjustments.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        I will give you concrete rates in the initial call, once I know what it is about. That way you get a number
        that fits your project instead of a range that means nothing.
    </p>

